#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Bring BackOtter test guests up to date, on every test host at once.

Usage: vm-update [--try] [--hosts HOST,...] [--jobs N] [--memory MB] GUEST...
       vm-update --status|--finish [--hosts HOST,...] GUEST...

Run on the host that has the guests (Chopin), from anywhere: GUEST is a
folder in vms/ next to this script's testvm/.  For each GUEST, vm-update

  1. checks that nothing uses the guest's images, here or on the other hosts
     (a QEMU program with them open, or a qcow2 overlay on top of them), and
     that each host's images are still the ones the last update (or the
     copy) left there: the SHA-256s recorded in vms/GUEST/image-state, and
     the same generation as here;
  2. boots the guest with vm-run --ram --keep (its vm-run-options, the
     passphrase typed when it has one), so that every write goes to an
     overlay beside the image, which itself is only read, once this host
     has memory to spare for the VM;
  3. runs update/in-guest.sh in it over SSH: the guest's own package manager
     (apt, dnf, pacman, zypper, xbps, apk, nixos-rebuild, pkg, syspatch and
     pkg_add, pkg on OmniOS, pkgman on Haiku), unattended, after pointing a
     guest that its builder froze at its distribution's live repositories;
     as root, or as the user named in vms/GUEST/ssh-user (Haiku's "user",
     which is uid 0: Haiku has no root);
  4. reboots it and waits for SSH again, so a broken kernel or boot loader
     stops here, then powers it off;
  5. copies the overlay (only the blocks the update wrote) to every other
     host, checks step 1 again on every host, and then on every host merges
     the overlay into the image (qemu-img commit), retakes the
     "fresh-install" snapshot and records the new SHA-256s and generation in
     image-state.

So the hosts' images stay identical in what the guest sees, while only the
update crosses the network.  (Not byte-identical: the snapshot tables carry
each host's own timestamps, hence image-state.)  A guest that nothing can
update (NetBSD: a release's base sets) is reported and left alone.

  --try         stop after step 4 and throw the overlay away: test that the
                guest updates and still boots, changing nothing
  --hosts HOST,...
                the other hosts (default: chopin and cinqcent, but this
                one); --hosts '' updates only this host's copy, after
                which the others no longer match it
  --jobs N      update N guests at once (default 3); each takes a 2 GB VM,
                its overlay on disk and a free SSH port, and waits for the
                memory it needs
  --memory MB   the VM's RAM instead of vm-run's 2048
  --status      only step 1's checks: whether the hosts agree, and what uses
                the images
  --finish      complete merges that step 5 left undone on some host (it
                says so, and keeps the overlay there for this)

Logs: vms/GUEST/update.log (what ran, with the guest's output) and
vms/GUEST/update-serial.log (the serial console).  Stopping vm-update
(Ctrl-C, or a hangup or TERM) stops its VMs and deletes their overlays,
changing nothing; merges already under way finish first.
"""

